\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces The LCM tower and the integrative-level correspondence. The divisor column confirms the doubling law $\tau (N_{\ell }) = 6\cdot 2^{\ell }$ of Theorem~\ref {thm:doubling}: each row's $\tau $ value is exactly twice the previous. The ``newly native'' entries are those families $d$ with $d\mid N_{\ell }$ but $d\nmid N_{\ell '}$ for every earlier tower level $N_{\ell '}$. By Proposition~\ref {prop:int-resolution}, a phenomenon whose Cardinal decomposition involves family set $\mathcal  {D}_{\ell }$ is minimally identifiable at $N_{\ell }^{\min }=\lcm  (\mathcal  {D}_{\ell })$; by Corollary~\ref {cor:level-log} its integrative level is $\ell = \log _{2}(\tau _{\ell }/6)$.}}{50}{table.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:int-resolution}{{6}{50}{The LCM tower and the integrative-level correspondence. The divisor column confirms the doubling law $\tau (N_{\ell }) = 6\cdot 2^{\ell }$ of Theorem~\ref {thm:doubling}: each row's $\tau $ value is exactly twice the previous. The ``newly native'' entries are those families $d$ with $d\mid N_{\ell }$ but $d\nmid N_{\ell '}$ for every earlier tower level $N_{\ell '}$. By Proposition~\ref {prop:int-resolution}, a phenomenon whose Cardinal decomposition involves family set $\mathcal {D}_{\ell }$ is minimally identifiable at $N_{\ell }^{\min }=\lcm (\mathcal {D}_{\ell })$; by Corollary~\ref {cor:level-log} its integrative level is $\ell = \log _{2}(\tau _{\ell }/6)$}{table.6}{}}
